cornerComplex -- form the corner complex
****************************************

  * Usage: 
        C = cornerComplex(T,c)
        C = cornerComplex(M,c,low,high)
  * Inputs:
      * T, a *note complex: (Complexes)Complex,, a (part of a) Tate resolution
        on a product of t projective spaces
      * c, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, cohomological degree of upper
        corner of the  last quadrant complex which is part of the corner
        complex
      * M, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, multi-graded module
        representing a sheaf F
      * low, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a multidegree
      * high, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a multidegree
  * Outputs:
      * C, a *note complex: (Complexes)Complex,, The corner complex

Description
===========

The call

cornerComplex(T,c)

forms the corner complex with corner c of a (part of a) Tate resolution T as
defined in Tate Resolutions on Products of Projective Spaces (see
http://arxiv.org/abs/1411.5724 ). The call

cornerComplex(M,c,low,high)

first computes the Tate resolution T of the sheaf F represented by M in the
range covering low to high and then takes the corner complex of T.

+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : (S,E) = productOfProjectiveSpaces{1,1}                              |
|                                                                         |
|o1 = (S, E)                                                              |
|                                                                         |
|o1 : Sequence                                                            |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : low = {-4,-4};high = {3,2};                                         |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : T1= (dual freeResolution( trim (ideal vars E)^2,LengthLimit=>8))[1];|
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : T2=freeResolution(coker upperCorner(T1,{4,3}),LengthLimit=>13)[7];  |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Finally, we can define T, the sufficient part of the Tate resolution:

+----------------------------------------------+
|i6 : T=trivialHomologicalTruncation (T2,-5,6);|
+----------------------------------------------+
